  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Chef, who is a chef by profession as well, has created NNN pies (enumerated 111 through NNN) and has placed them in a row. Now he wants to taste all the pies, but by following a rather peculiar order of tasting.\n\nAt each step, he chooses (uniformly randomly and independently) either the current leftmost pie or the rightmost pie, and eats that pie.\n\nFor example, if N=7N = 7N=7, then one valid order of tasting the pies is (1,2,7,3,4,6,5)(1, 2, 7, 3, 4, 6, 5)(1,2,7,3,4,6,5). However, (1,2,7,6,4,5,3)(1, 2, 7, 6, 4, 5, 3)(1,2,7,6,4,5,3) is not a valid order.\n\nChef can't wait to taste all the pies, so for each iii (1≤i≤N)(1 \\leq i \\leq N)(1≤i≤N), he wants to know the expected value of the number of steps he'll need to eat the ithi^{th}ith pie.\n\nIt can be shown that each expected value can be expressed as a fraction PQ\\frac{P}{Q}QP​. You should compute P⋅Q−1P \\cdot Q^{-1}P⋅Q−1 modulo 109+710^9+7109+7, where Q−1Q^{-1}Q−1 denotes the modular multiplicative inverse of QQQ under 109+710^9+7109+7\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input contains a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases. The description of TTT test cases follows.\nEach test case consists of a single line containing one integer NNN — the number of pies.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, print a single line containing NNN space-separated integers, the ithi^{th}ith of which is the expected number of steps Chef needs to eat the ithi^{th}ith pie.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤101 \\leq T \\leq 101≤T≤10",
      "1≤N≤1051 \\leq N \\leq 10^51≤N≤105"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "2\n3\n7",
        "output": "750000007 500000006 750000007\n859375008 593750008 390625008 312500008 390625008 593750008 859375008",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111:\nThere are 333 ways of eating the 1st1^{st}1st pie:\n\n(1)(1)(1) : The probability of this ordering is 12\\frac{1}{2}21​ and number of steps is 111\n(3,1)(3, 1)(3,1) : The probability of this ordering is 14\\frac{1}{4}41​ and number of steps is 222\n(3,2,1)(3, 2, 1)(3,2,1) : The probability of this ordering is 14\\frac{1}{4}41​ and number of steps is 333\n\nTherefore, the expected number of steps for the 1st1^{st}1st pie is 12⋅1+14⋅2+14⋅3=74\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1 + \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 2 + \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 3 = \\frac{7}{4}21​⋅1+41​⋅2+41​⋅3=47​\nThere are 444 ways of eating the 2nd2^{nd}2nd pie:\n\n(1,2)(1, 2)(1,2) : The probability of this ordering is 14\\frac{1}{4}41​ and number of steps is 222\n(3,2)(3, 2)(3,2) : The probability of this ordering is 14\\frac{1}{4}41​ and number of steps is 222\n(1,3,2)(1, 3, 2)(1,3,2) : The probability of this ordering is 14\\frac{1}{4}41​ and number of steps is 333\n(3,1,2)(3, 1, 2)(3,1,2) : The probability of this ordering is 14\\frac{1}{4}41​ and number of steps is 333\n\nTherefore, the expected number of steps for the 2nd2^{nd}2nd pie is 14⋅2+14⋅2+14⋅3+14⋅3=52\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 2 + \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 2 + \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 3 + \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 3 = \\frac{5}{2}41​⋅2+41​⋅2+41​⋅3+41​⋅3=25​"
      }
    ]
  },
